{"step_index":279,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:14:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-17T16:14:38Z\nCompleted At: 2026-08-17T16:14:38Z\nFile Path: `file:///google/src/cloud/gauravrawat/summarize_latest_chats/google3/hr_agentic_solution/brd_v2.md`\nTotal Lines: 173\nTotal Bytes: 17521\nShowing lines 1 to 173\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Business Requirements Document (BRD) - HR Agentic Solution (MVP 1 - Revised)\n2: \n3: ## Executive Summary\n4: \n5: The **HR Agentic Solution** is a secure, enterprise AI-driven virtual assistant designed to provide employees with immediate, conversational access to HR services. By orchestrating workflows across core enterprise systems (WorkWeek HCM, ServiceImmediately ITSM/HRSD) and retrieving answers from approved HR policies, the assistant automates routine inquiries and facilitates self-service transactions.\n6: \n7: To ensure enterprise governance and zero-trust security during the MVP 1 phase, the solution deploys an **API Gateway Mock Identity Assertion Layer** (signed JWT session claims) that binds conversational turns to authenticated employee profiles. The system enforces traceably bounded execution, authenticates automation origins for all backend operations, and dynamically validates interactions with probabilistic safety benchmarks to protect against prompt injection and data leaks.\n8: \n9: ---\n10: \n11: ## 1. Project Objectives\n12: \n13: * **Deflect Tier 1 Inquiries (40% Target):** Reduce routine HR and IT helpdesk ticket volume by at least 40% within the first six months.\n14:   * *Baseline Assumption:* 5,000 Tier-1 inquiries/month (60% IT Helpdesk, 40% HR Inquiries).\n15:   * *Deflection Definition:* A session is counted as deflected if the user resolves their inquiry via grounded policy Q&A (without opening a ticket within 24 hours) or successfully completes a self-service transaction without human escalation.\n16:   * *Target Volume:* Deflect at least 2,000 inquiries/month by Month 6.\n17: * **Streamline HR & IT Transactions:** Enable employees to perform core self-service actions (leave submission in work days, incident ticket status checks, and ticket creation) conversationally with mandatory Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) confirmation cards.\n18: * **Validate Cross-System Orchestration:** Demonstrate resilient chaining across HR policies, WorkWeek, and ServiceImmediately with forward error recovery and Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) tracking before wider enterprise rollout.\n19: * **Ensure Enterprise AI Governance:** Maintain 100% audit visibility over tool invocations, deployment versions, and authorized capability boundaries.\n20: * **Mitigate AI Risks:** Achieve high-precision safety enforcement (&gt;= 99.5% precision/recall on safety benchmarks) and 0% ungrounded hallucinations on approved benchmark test suites via deterministic retrieval guardrails and strict PII redaction.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. Project Scope (MVP 1)\n25: \n26: ### 2.1. Functional Scope (In-Scope for MVP 1)\n27: \n28: * **Conversational User Interface (UI):** Web-based chat client equipped with a test persona switcher injecting signed mock identity assertions (`X-Authenticated-User`), streaming response tokens, and interactive confirmation cards.\n29: * **Policy & Informational Queries (RAG):** Answering employee inquiries derived strictly from ingested, approved static documents with exact deep-link citations.\n30: * **Employee Self-Service Transactions (WorkWeek HCM):**\n31:   * *Read Actions:* Real-time retrieval of employee profile details and accrued/used/remaining leave balances (in work days).\n32:   * *Write Actions:* Updating personal home address and phone number; submitting new leave requests with business calendar validation.\n33: * **Support Desk Management (ServiceImmediately ITSM/HRSD):**\n34:   * *Read Actions:* Querying incident ticket status, priority, category, assignee, and public comment history.\n35:   * *Write Actions:* Creating new incident tickets (P1-P4), posting follow-up comments to active tickets, and canceling tickets in 'New' status.\n36: * **Resilient Cross-System Orchestration:** Multi-step chaining (e.g., verifying remote work eligibility, checking WorkWeek profile, and generating hardware procurement tickets in ServiceImmediately) with explicit user confirmation and DLQ failure handling.\n37: \n38: ### 2.2. Data Scope (In-Scope for MVP 1)\n39: \n40: * **Static HR Policies:** Curated repository of approved documents (PDF/Text) covering Leave Policies, Expense Guidelines, Remote Work Guidelines, and Code of Conduct.\n41: * **WorkWeek Data Elements:**\n42:   * *Employee Profile:* Employee ID, Full Name, Email, Department, Role, Manager ID, Hire Date.\n43:   * *Contact Info:* Personal Address (street, city, state, postal code), Personal Phone Number (E.164).\n44:   * *Leave Data:* Accrued, Used, and Remaining balances for \"Vacation\" and \"Sick\" categories (tracked in work days).\n45: * **ServiceImmediately Data Elements:**\n46:   * *Incident Record:* Ticket ID, Short Description, Detailed Description, Category, Priority (1-Critical, 2-High, 3-Moderate, 4-Low), State (New, In Progress, On Hold, Resolved, Closed, Canceled), Assignee, Comment Timeline.\n47: \n48: ### 2.3. Out of Scope for MVP 1\n49: \n50: * Integration with production Identity Providers (Okta/Azure AD/Ping) — simulated via signed Gateway assertions in MVP 1.\n51: * Direct processing of core payroll adjustments, formal compensation planning, or performance appraisal records.\n52: * Automated badge/facility physical access provisioning and email routing automation (UC-2.3 Relocation is deferred to Phase 2).\n53: * Multi-lingual natural language translation.\n54: * Voice/telephony IVR integrations.\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 3. MVP 1 Use Cases and Interaction Specifications\n59: \n60: | Use Case ID | Category | Triggering User Prompt (Example) | Systems Involved | System Actions & Expected Behavior |\n61: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n62: | **UC-1.1** | Policy Q&A | *\"What is the company's bereavement leave policy?\"* or *\"Can remote employees expense monitors?\"* | Policy Repository | Retrieve relevant policy chunks; generate grounded response with clickable citation deep links. Refuse out-of-domain queries. |\n63: | **UC-1.2** | HR Self-Service | *\"How many days of PTO do I currently have accrued?\"* or *\"Submit a vacation request for this Thursday and Friday.\"* | WorkWeek (HCM) | 1. Query real-time leave balance in work days.<br>2. Calculate business days (excluding holidays/weekends).<br>3. Present User Confirmation Card.<br>4. Submit request to WorkWeek in 'Submitted for Approval' state. |\n64: | **UC-1.3** | IT Incident Mgmt | *\"What is the status of ticket INC102938?\"* or *\"Create an IT ticket for intermittent VPN disconnections.\"* | ServiceImmediately (ITSM) | 1. Query ticket status/timeline.<br>2. Check for duplicate tickets submitted within 15 minutes.<br>3. Create incident ticket with mapped category/priority. |\n65: | **UC-2.1** | Cross-System: Equipment Procurement | *\"I am a remote employee. Can you check my eligibility and request a home office monitor for me?\"* | Policy Docs, WorkWeek, ServiceImmediately | 1. Verify remote policy stipend.<br>2. Check employee location/status in WorkWeek.<br>3. Render Confirmation Card with item specs and shipping address.<br>4. Upon user confirmation, create procurement request ticket in ServiceImmediately. |\n66: | **UC-2.2** | Cross-System: Medical Leave | *\"I need to take short-term medical leave starting next Monday. What is the process and can you submit it?\"* | Policy Docs, WorkWeek, ServiceImmediately | 1. Quote medical leave procedure and required documentation.<br>2. Present confirmation card and submit Leave of Absence in WorkWeek.<br>3. Attempt to open notification ticket in ServiceImmediately.<br>4. *Resilience:* If ticket creation fails, retain WorkWeek leave, emit an alert to the HR Ops DLQ, and provide user with the WorkWeek confirmation reference ID. |\n67: | **UC-2.3** | Cross-System: Relocation | *Deferred to Phase 2* | — | *Deferred to Phase 2 due to physical access badge and payroll relocation dependencies.* |\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 4. Functional Requirements\n72: \n73: ### 4.1. AI Governance & Security Infrastructure\n74: \n75: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Description & Specification |\n76: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n77: | **FR-1.1** | **Capability & Tool Governance** | Enforce strict registry boundaries on authorized tools (WorkWeek adapter, ServiceImmediately adapter, Vector Search). Block unauthorized system or network invocations. |\n78: | **FR-1.2** | **Verification of Request Origin** | Audit records must capture the verified caller identity (`employee_id`, `role`) alongside the automation execution ID. |\n79: | **FR-1.3** | **Conversation Safety & Guardrails** | **Input Guardrails:** Intercept prompt injections, jailbreaks, and off-domain queries before execution (&lt; 300ms overhead).<br>**Output Guardrails:** Filter hallucinated policy statements, toxic text, and unredacted sensitive data. |\n80: | **FR-1.4** | **PII / SPII Redaction** | Automatically detect and mask Sensitive Personally Identifiable Information (SSNs, home addresses, phone numbers, banking details) from persistent logs. |\n81: | **FR-1.5** | **Orchestration-Level RBAC** | Enforce RBAC at the Agent Orchestration layer using verified Gateway identity claims. Ensure users can only query/mutate their own profile and ticket records. |\n82: \n83: ### 4.2. Core Capabilities & Dialog Management\n84: \n85: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Description & Specification |\n86: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n87: | **FR-2.1** | **Natural Language Understanding** | Accurately parse intents across synonyms, typos, and conversational phrasing with contextual disambiguation. |\n88: | **FR-2.2** | **Session State & Memory** | Maintain multi-turn conversational context within an active session. Automatically purge session context upon timeout (30 minutes of inactivity) or explicit user `/reset`. |\n89: | **FR-2.3** | **Human-in-the-Loop Confirmation** | Present an interactive summary confirmation card before executing any state-mutating transaction (leave booking, address edit, ticket creation). |\n90: \n91: ### 4.3. WorkWeek Integration (HCM)\n92: \n93: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Description & Operations |\n94: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n95: | **FR-3.1** | **Gateway Delegated Authorization** | Chat gateway injects a signed JWT with `employee_id` and `role`. WorkWeek adapter scopes all read/write operations strictly to the asserted caller. |\n96: | **FR-3.2** | **Core HCM Operations** | • **Profile Query:** Retrieve name, email, department, role, manager, hire date, home address, and phone number.<br>• **Contact Update:** Update personal address and phone number following schema validation.<br>• **Leave Balance Query:** Retrieve accrued, used, and remaining balances in **work days** for Vacation and Sick categories.<br>• **Submit Leave Request:** Submit time off specifying start date, end date, leave category, and calculated work days. Default status is `Submitted for Manager Approval`. |\n97: | **FR-3.3** | **Validation Guardrails** | • **Balance Constraint:** Reject requests exceeding remaining accrued days.<br>• **Business Calendar Validation:** Exclude weekend days and declared company holidays from requested durations.<br>• **Temporal Consistency:** Reject past dates or start dates after end dates.<br>• **Format Validation:** Validate E.164 phone formats and postal codes. |\n98: | **FR-3.4** | **Real-Time Fetch & Circuit Breaker** | Fetch dynamic balances in real time on every query (no AI-layer caching). Apply a 5-second timeout with exponential backoff (max 2 retries). If WorkWeek is offline, return a graceful degradation notice while keeping Policy Q&A operational. |\n99: \n100: ### 4.4. ServiceImmediately Integration (ITSM/HRSD)\n101: \n102: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Description & Operations |\n103: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n104: | **FR-4.1** | **Auditable Ticket Management** | Log all ticket creations with caller identity, automation agent ID, and idempotency keys to prevent duplicate creation. |\n105: | **FR-4.2** | **Ticket Lifecycle Operations** | • **Query Ticket:** Retrieve status, category, priority, assignee, short description, and comment timeline.<br>• **Create Incident:** Open new incident specifying category, short description, detailed description, and priority (P1-P4).<br>• **Add Comment:** Append user update notes to the ticket activity timeline.<br>• **Cancel Ticket:** Allow end-users to transition tickets to 'Canceled' only if ticket is currently in 'New' state. Status updates to 'Resolved' or 'Closed' are restricted to technician roles. |\n106: | **FR-4.3** | **Deduplication & Quality Guardrails** | • **Deduplication:** Scan tickets created within the past 15 minutes by the same caller. If semantic similarity on short description &gt; 0.85, prompt user to confirm or append to existing ticket.<br>• **Priority Validation:** Verify P1/Critical criteria before assigning top-tier priority tags. |\n107: \n108: ### 4.5. Policy Document Q&A (RAG)\n109: \n110: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Description & Specification |\n111: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n112: | **FR-5.1** | **Document Ingestion & Indexing** | Ingest, chunk, and generate embeddings for approved HR policy PDFs/documents. |\n113: | **FR-5.2** | **Strict Grounding** | Generate answers derived strictly from retrieved context chunks. If document evidence is insufficient, explicitly state inability to answer. |\n114: | **FR-5.3** | **Clickable Source Citations** | Return metadata with every answer, including document title, section heading, and verified deep link URL. |\n115: | **FR-5.4** | **Domain Containment** | Intercept and refuse non-HR/non-IT questions (e.g., coding, general knowledge, personal advice). |\n116: | **FR-5.5** | **Knowledge Sync SLA** | Reflect document updates in the Knowledge Base within **&lt;= 1 hour** via webhook/CDC triggers, supported by a manual admin sync endpoint and a daily 24-hour batch refresh. |\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. Non-Functional Requirements (NFR)\n121: \n122: ### 5.1. Security, Privacy & Compliance\n123: \n124: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Specification |\n125: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n126: | **NFR-1.1** | **Interaction Safety** | Filter malicious prompt injections, indirect RAG injections, and harmful outputs (&gt;= 99.5% benchmark accuracy). |\n127: | **NFR-1.2** | **Immutable Audit Logging** | Log all system interactions, tool calls, and blocked safety events with timestamp, caller ID, and action metadata. |\n128: | **NFR-1.3** | **Data Protection & Compliance** | Mask all SPII/PII in log stores; enforce TLS 1.3 in transit and AES-256 at rest. Support GDPR Right to be Forgotten purge scripts. |\n129: \n130: ### 5.2. Performance & Scalability\n131: \n132: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Target Latency & Throughput Targets |\n133: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n134: | **NFR-2.1** | **Conversational Latency SLAs** | • **Policy Q&A (RAG):** p50 &lt; 1.5s, p95 &lt; 3.0s.<br>• **Single-System Transactions:** p50 &lt; 2.0s, p95 &lt; 4.0s.<br>• **Chained Orchestration (UC-2.x):** p50 &lt; 4.0s, p95 &lt; 7.0s with token streaming.<br>• **Safety Scanning Overhead:** &lt; 300ms per conversational turn. |\n135: | **NFR-2.2** | **Availability & Capacity** | 99.9% uptime SLA; designed to support 50 peak concurrent user sessions and 5,000 monthly transactions in MVP 1. |\n136: | **NFR-2.3** | **Asynchronous Tool Execution** | Backend adapter calls and external lookups execute asynchronously without blocking conversational streaming. |\n137: \n138: ### 5.3. Quality & Grounding\n139: \n140: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Benchmark Target |\n141: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n142: | **NFR-3.1** | **Answer Accuracy & Grounding** | Achieve &gt;= 95% accuracy on predefined HR policy evaluation sets with 0% ungrounded hallucinations. |\n143: \n144: ### 5.4. Resilience & Error Handling\n145: \n146: | Requirement ID | Requirement Name | Specification |\n147: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n148: | **NFR-4.1** | **Graceful Degradation** | Display friendly, actionable user messages on backend failures without exposing internal stack traces or API keys. |\n149: | **NFR-4.2** | **Idempotent Retries** | Implement exponential backoff with jitter on network timeouts. Pass `Idempotency-Key` headers on all state-mutating requests. |\n150: | **NFR-4.3** | **Forward SAGA Recovery (DLQ)** | In multi-system flows, if a subsequent step fails, retain prior successful steps, emit an event to the HR Operations Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ), and provide the user with the successful transaction reference ID. |\n151: \n152: ---\n153: \n154: ## 6. MVP 1 Implementation Architecture & Constraints\n155: \n156: * **Gateway Mock Identity Assertion Layer:** To satisfy zero-trust RBAC and origin verification without blocking on enterprise Okta/SSO integration, the Chat UI includes a test persona selector that issues signed JWT assertion headers (`X-Authenticated-User`) to the API Gateway. The Gateway verifies the signature, and the Agent Orchestrator enforces caller data boundaries.\n157: * **Backend Credentials:** Backend adapters connect to WorkWeek and ServiceImmediately sandbox instances using dedicated service accounts scoped by the API Gateway's asserted caller context.\n158: * **Tenancy Scope:** Deployed as a single-tenant instance for MVP 1 evaluation.\n159: \n160: ---\n161: \n162: ## 7. Success and Evaluation Criteria\n163: \n164: | Evaluation Category | Success Metric / Criterion | Target Benchmark |\n165: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n166: | **Deflection Rate** | Tier-1 tickets deflected via self-service and policy answers | 40% reduction (2,000 tickets/month deflected by Month 6) |\n167: | **Policy Q&A Quality** | Grounded precision, recall, and citation validity | &gt;= 95% Accuracy on test benchmark; 0% Hallucination |\n168: | **Transaction Integrity** | Correct execution of leave submissions and ticket operations | 100% Correctness; 0 duplicate transactions |\n169: | **Cross-System Chaining** | Successful execution of UC-2.1 and UC-2.2 with DLQ fallback | 100% pass on test suite scenarios |\n170: | **Safety & Guardrails** | Interception of jailbreaks, injections, and toxic prompts | &gt;= 99.5% Detection rate; &lt; 1% False Positive rate |\n171: | **Response Latency** | End-to-end turnaround across RAG and tool calls | Policy Q&A p95 &lt; 3.0s; Chained workflows p95 &lt; 7.0s |\n172: | **Audit Traceability** | Log coverage of all system actions and blocked requests | 100% Log Coverage with caller identity binding |\n173: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":280,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:14:38Z","content":"# Architectural Stress-Test & Gap Analysis: HR Agentic Solution BRD (MVP 1 - Revised)\n\n**Document Under Review:** `HR Agentic Solution BRD (MVP 1 - Revised & Approved)`  \n**Role:** Principal Product Manager & Principal Systems Architect  \n**Evaluation Phase:** Final Pre-Implementation Gate  \n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe **Revised BRD (MVP 1)** has successfully resolved the primary architectural contradictions present in the draft version (namely, the identity/SSO contradiction, uncalibrated deflection metrics, SAGA rollback ambiguity, and unit mismatches). \n\nHowever, before engineering begins Sprint 1, this stress-test identifies **secondary operational gaps, distributed state race conditions, and integration edge cases** that must be codified in technical designs to avoid runtime defects.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Clarity & Ambiguity Check\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              REVISED BRD AMBIGUITY AUDIT                               │\n├─────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Section & Item          │ Current Formulation           │ Engineering Ambiguity / Gap  │\n├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ FR-2.1: Disambiguation  │ \"Accurately parse intents...  │ ⚠️ No threshold for multi-   │\n│                         │ contextual disambiguation\"    │ intent queries (e.g., PTO +  │\n│                         │                               │ Ticket status in one prompt) │\n├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ FR-4.3: Priority Verify │ \"Verify P1/Critical criteria  │ ⚠️ Subjective heuristic: Who │\n│                         │ before assigning top-tier\"    │ defines P1 criteria? Can the │\n│                         │                               │ LLM downgrade a user's P1?   │\n├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ FR-3.2: Holiday Exclude │ \"Exclude declared company     │ ⚠️ Underspecified multi-loc: │\n│                         │ holidays from durations\"      │ Which calendar (caller's     │\n│                         │                               │ state/country vs. HQ)?       │\n├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ NFR-2.2: Capacity       │ \"50 peak concurrent sessions; │ ⚠️ Concurrency definition:   │\n│                         │ 5,000 monthly transactions\"   │ Is 50 CCU concurrent HTTP    │\n│                         │                               │ connections or open sockets? │\n└─────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Specific Findings:\n1. **Multi-Intent & Compound Prompts (FR-2.1):**\n   * *Ambiguity:* How should the orchestrator handle compound prompts, such as: *\"What is the sick leave policy, and also check my balance and submit 1 day off for tomorrow\"*?\n   * *Architectural Requirement:* The BRD lacks an intent decomposition rule. It must specify whether the agent executes sequentially, asks the user to split their request, or executes read-only RAG while gating state mutations.\n2. **Multi-Regional Business Holiday Calendars (FR-3.3):**\n   * *Ambiguity:* The document specifies excluding company holidays, but does not state how geographic calendar resolution occurs (e.g., US Federal vs. UK Bank Holidays vs. Regional State Floating Holidays).\n   * *Resolution:* The calendar engine must resolve holidays using the `country` and `location` claims in the `X-Authenticated-User` JWT.\n3. **P1/Critical Priority Override Authority (FR-4.3):**\n   * *Ambiguity:* *\"Verify P1/Critical criteria before assigning top-tier priority tags.\"*\n   * *Conflict:* If an employee selects \"Critical\" for an issue the LLM classifies as \"Moderate\", does the agent silently downgrade the priority, reject the submission, or warn the user? *(Recommendation: Warn and prompt for confirmation; never silently alter priority).*\n\n---\n\n## 2. Missing Functional Scope & Edge Cases\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Edge_Case_Matrix [\"Unhandle Scenario Flowchart\"]\n        A[User Requests 10 Days PTO] --> B{Check WorkWeek Balance}\n        B -->|Sufficient| C[Render HITL Confirmation Card]\n        C --> D[User Leaves Tab Open for 45 mins]\n        D --> E{User Clicks Confirm}\n        E -->|Session Expired (30m)| F[Token / Context Stale Failure!]\n        E -->|Balance Changed in Backend| G[Overdraft Transaction Failure!]\n    end\n    style F fill:#ffebee,stroke:#f44336;\n    style G fill:#ffebee,stroke:#f44336;\n```\n\n### 2.1. State & Lifecycle Edge Cases\n* **Stale HITL Confirmation Cards:**\n  * *Scenario:* The agent renders a confirmation card for a 3-day leave request. The employee leaves the browser tab open for 2 hours. In the interim, another leave is approved in WorkWeek, reducing their balance to 1 day. The employee then clicks `[Confirm & Submit]`.\n  * *Missing Requirement:* The confirmation handler must re-validate balances and business calendar constraints at execution time (`POST /actions/confirm`), rather than relying solely on the pre-check state.\n* **Concurrent Browser Tabs / Multi-Device Sessions:**\n  * *Scenario:* An employee opens the Chat UI in two separate tabs. Tab A starts a leave submission; Tab B starts an IT ticket creation.\n  * *Missing Requirement:* Session state in Redis must support action-level concurrency locking (`action_id` mutex) to prevent race conditions from overwriting conversational context.\n* **Ticket Comment Timeline Pagination & Token Flooding:**\n  * *Scenario:* An incident ticket has 50+ historical updates/comments (e.g., automated system logs).\n  * *Missing Requirement:* Fetching the complete timeline in FR-4.2 will exhaust LLM context windows. The adapter must truncate and return only the **last 5 public comments** unless specifically requested.\n\n### 2.2. Boundary & Input Extremes\n* **Partial / Half-Day Leave Increments:**\n  * *Gap:* The BRD specifies integer work days (e.g., \"2.0 work days\"). It does not define whether employees can request half-days (`0.5 work days` / 4 hours) or how the business calendar engine handles partial-day deductions.\n* **Same-Day / Retroactive Leave Requests:**\n  * *Gap:* FR-3.3 rejects \"past dates\". What is the rule for \"same-day\" sick leave submitted during the work shift?\n* **Ticket Attachment Handling:**\n  * *Gap:* When reporting IT incidents (e.g., VPN disconnects in UC-1.3), users often paste error screenshots or stack traces. The BRD does not specify handling for image paste or file attachments in the chat UI.\n\n### 2.3. Permission & Role Edge Cases\n* **Manager vs. Employee Impersonation in Sandbox:**\n  * *Gap:* If a manager persona (`Alice Brown - Manager`) is selected, can she approve pending requests for her direct reports through the chat assistant? Currently, only individual self-service is specified.\n* **Confidential HRSD Inquiries:**\n  * *Gap:* If an employee queries tickets, what prevents the agent from displaying confidential HR investigation or grievance tickets? The ServiceImmediately adapter must filter queries to `category != \"HR_Confidential\"`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Non-Functional Requirements (NFR) Assessment\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                NFR STRESS-TEST EVALUATION                              │\n├───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Dimension                     │ Specification in BRD    │ Stress-Test Assessment       │\n├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Latency: Policy RAG           │ p50 < 1.5s, p95 < 3.0s  │ 🟢 Feasible via Vector Search│\n├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Latency: Chained Workflows    │ p50 < 4.0s, p95 < 7.0s  │ 🟡 Tight: Requires async     │\n│                               │                         │ token streaming & parallel DB│\n├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Cold-Start Latency            │ Unspecified             │ 🔴 Blocker: Cloud Run cold   │\n│                               │                         │ starts can add 2-4s latency  │\n├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Data Growth & Audit Scale     │ 5,000 tx/month          │ 🟢 Manageable (<10GB/year)   │\n├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Disaster Recovery (DR)        │ RTO <= 15m, RPO <= 5m   │ 🟢 Achievable with Multi-Zone│\n└───────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Critical NFR Gaps:\n1. **Serverless Cold-Start Latency:**\n   * On Cloud Run, cold-starting containers loading Python ML/LangGraph libraries can add **2,000ms – 4,000ms**, violating the $p95 < 3.0\\text{s}$ SLA for the first request.\n   * *Requirement:* Mandate a minimum instance count (`min_instance_count = 1`) on the orchestrator and gateway services.\n2. **Rate Limiting & Anti-Scraping Policies:**\n   * The BRD does not specify rate-limiting thresholds for individual employees. A compromised client could scrape internal policy documents by bombarding the RAG endpoint.\n   * *Requirement:* Enforce maximum 20 requests/minute per `employee_id` at the API Gateway.\n\n---\n\n## 4. System Dependencies & Technical Risks\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Dependency_Risks [\"Technical Risk Radar\"]\n        direction TB\n        R1[\"1. Upstream Sandbox Drift\\n(Schema changes between Sandbox & Prod)\"]\n        R2[\"2. Vector Embedding Deprecation\\n(text-embedding-005 version updates)\"]\n        R3[\"3. LLM Non-Determinism in HITL\\n(Parsing date strings across locales)\"]\n        R4[\"4. Pub/Sub DLQ Consumer Starvation\\n(Unmonitored dead-letter queue)\"]\n    end\n    style R1 fill:#ffebee,stroke:#f44336;\n    style R2 fill:#fff3e0,stroke:#ff9800;\n    style R3 fill:#fff3e0,stroke:#ff9800;\n    style R4 fill:#e8f5e9,stroke:#4caf50;\n```\n\n1. **Date/Time Locality & ISO Parsing Risk:**\n   * Users enter localized date formats (e.g., `04/09/2026` = April 9 in US vs. September 4 in UK). \n   * *Mitigation:* The NLU layer must resolve ambiguous dates using the caller's `country` claim from the signed JWT, and the Confirmation Card must explicitly render the full written date (e.g., *\"Friday, September 4, 2026\"*).\n2. **RAG Embedding Model Drift:**\n   * If the embedding model (`text-embedding-005`) is updated or re-indexed with a different dimension, the Vector Search index must be rebuilt atomically using blue/green deployment to avoid breaking live search.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Scope Creep & Feasibility (MoSCoW)\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              FINAL MoSCoW SCOPE GATES                                  │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 🟢 MUST-HAVE (Sprint 1 - 2 Critical Path)                                              │\n│  • Gateway Mock Identity Assertion (Signed JWT persona injection).                     │\n│  • Grounded Policy Q&A RAG with exact deep-link citation rendering.                    │\n│  • Real-time Leave balance lookup & Leave submission (work days, holiday exclusions).  │\n│  • Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) confirmation cards for all state mutations.                │\n│  • Incident ticket creation (P1-P4) & status lookup with caller scoping.               │\n│  • Input/Output Safety guardrails & Sensitive Data Protection (DLP) log masking.       │\n│  • Forward SAGA Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) logging for partial multi-system failures.     │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 🟡 SHOULD-HAVE (Sprint 3 - 4 Enhancements)                                             │\n│  • 15-minute duplicate ticket similarity pre-screening.                                │\n│  • End-user ticket cancellation in 'New' state.                                        │\n│  • Contact information updates (personal address & phone).                             │\n│  • Automated 30-day audit log purge / GDPR compliance endpoint.                        │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 🔵 COULD-HAVE (Phase 2 - Post-MVP Backlog)                                             │\n│  • Multi-intent compound prompt decomposition.                                         │\n│  • Half-day (0.5 work day) leave increments.                                           │\n│  • Manager approval workflows for direct reports.                                      │\n│  • Image paste & screenshot attachment parsing for IT tickets.                         │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 🔴 WON'T-HAVE (Explicitly Excluded)                                                    │\n│  • Full enterprise Okta SSO (simulated via Gateway JWT in MVP 1).                      │\n│  • UC-2.3 Relocation & Facilities badge physical access provisioning.                   │\n│  • Payroll adjustments, compensation planning, and performance reviews.                │\n│  • Multi-lingual translation & Voice IVR telephony.                                    │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Clarifying Questions & Acceptance Criteria\n\n### 6.1. Prioritized Questions to Unblock Sprint 1\n\n1. **Partial-Day Leave Requests:**  \n   * *Question:* Does WorkWeek support half-day increments (`0.5 work days` / 4 hours) in MVP 1, or must all requests be integer days (`1.0, 2.0, 3.0`)?  \n   * *Recommendation:* Restrict to integer work days for MVP 1; defer half-days to Phase 2.\n2. **Ambiguous Date Interpretation:**  \n   * *Question:* How should the NLU engine handle ambiguous numeric dates (e.g., `03/04/2026`) when locale is not explicitly typed?  \n   * *Recommendation:* Default to caller's `country` claim (`US = MM/DD/YYYY`, `UK/EU = DD/MM/YYYY`) and always display full written month names on confirmation cards.\n3. **Compound Multi-Intent Prompts:**  \n   * *Question:* When a user asks a policy question and requests a transaction in a single prompt, should the system answer the policy and display the card simultaneously, or force a two-step dialog?  \n   * *Recommendation:* Answer the policy question first and append the confirmation card below the answer in a single combined turn.\n\n---\n\n### 6.2. Sample Acceptance Criteria (Gherkin Format)\n\n#### Scenario 1: Ambiguous Date Localization & Written Month Confirmation\n```gherkin\nFeature: Date Localization & Disambiguation\n\n  Scenario: User enters numeric date string in US locale\n    Given the authenticated caller is \"Jane Doe\" with location \"US\"\n    When the user prompts \"Book vacation for 09/04/2026 to 09/08/2026\"\n    Then the agent must interpret the dates as \"September 4, 2026\" to \"September 8, 2026\"\n    And the rendered Confirmation Card must display:\n      | Field | Formatted Value |\n      | Start Date | Friday, September 4, 2026 |\n      | End Date   | Tuesday, September 8, 2026 |\n      | Total Days | 2.0 Work Days (Labor Day excluded) |\n    And the user must be prompted to verify the written dates before submission.\n```\n\n#### Scenario 2: Stale Confirmation Card Re-Validation at Mutation Time\n```gherkin\nFeature: HITL Transaction Re-Validation\n\n  Scenario: Employee attempts to confirm leave after backend balance has changed\n    Given the employee was shown a confirmation card for 3.0 days of vacation\n    And the employee's remaining balance was 4.0 days at the time of display\n    When another system updates the employee's balance to 1.0 day while the card is open\n    And the employee clicks \"[Confirm & Submit]\"\n    Then the mutation endpoint must re-query WorkWeek balances\n    And the agent must reject the submission with:\n      \"\"\"\n      Unable to complete request: Your remaining vacation balance has changed to 1.0 day. \n      Please adjust your requested duration.\n      \"\"\"\n    And no write operation must be dispatched to WorkWeek.\n```\n\n#### Scenario 3: Compound Policy Inquiry & Transactional Booking\n```gherkin\nFeature: Multi-Intent Compound Prompt Handling\n\n  Scenario: User asks for policy rules and requests leave in a single turn\n    Given the employee has 10.0 days of Vacation balance\n    When the user prompts \"What is the bereavement policy, and also book vacation for next Friday?\"\n    Then the agent must retrieve the Bereavement policy and cite the official section\n    And the agent must calculate 1.0 work day for next Friday\n    And the response must contain:\n      | Section | Content |\n      | Top Text | Grounded Bereavement policy answer with citation deep link |\n      | Bottom Component | Interactive Vacation Confirmation Card for 1.0 work day |\n    And the leave transaction must remain pending until user confirms.\n```\n\n#### Scenario 4: DLQ Forward Recovery Event Schema Validation\n```gherkin\nFeature: Dead-Letter Queue Forward Resilience\n\n  Scenario: Verifying Cloud Pub/Sub DLQ payload structure on downstream failure\n    Given the employee confirmed a 5-day Medical Leave request\n    When WorkWeek records the Leave of Absence (Ref: \"LOA-8821\")\n    But ServiceImmediately returns HTTP 503 after 2 retries\n    Then the system must publish a Cloud Pub/Sub message to \"hr-ops-dead-letter-topic\"\n    And the published message must match the schema:\n      | Key | Expected Value |\n      | workflow_name | UC-2.2-MedicalLeave |\n      | primary_reference_id | LOA-8821 |\n      | employee_id | EMP-1042 |\n      | failed_step | ServiceImmediately_CreateITRoutingTicket |\n    And the user must receive a confirmation with WorkWeek Ref \"LOA-8821\" and HR Ops alert notice.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sign-Off & Implementation Readiness\n\n| Review Area | Architecture Status | Sign-Off Gate |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| **Identity & Auth Specification** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **GCP Cloud Run & Vertex AI Topology** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **API Data Contracts & Idempotency** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **Safety Guardrails & DLP Redaction** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **MoSCoW Backlog & Agile User Stories** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |"}
